THOUGHT 1: The detection call likely failed because I queried many object types at once, which can cause the underlying service to error. I'll try a more robust approach: run detection one object at a time (catching exceptions), and if none succeed, fall back to sliding_window_detection to search the image with patches. The code will display any annotated results and print the returned boxes so we can proceed from the successful detection (or patches) next.

ACTION 1:
```python
# ACTION 1: Robust detection retry (single-object queries) with fallback to sliding window search
objects_to_try = ["gauge", "dial", "meter", "scale", "thermometer", "ruler"]

found = False
for obj in objects_to_try:
    try:
        print(f"Trying detection for: '{obj}'")
        annotated_img, boxes = detection(image_1, [obj])
        display(annotated_img.annotated_image)
        print(f"Returned boxes for '{obj}': {boxes}\n")
        if boxes:
            print(f"Detected something for '{obj}'. Stopping further attempts.")
            found = True
            break
    except Exception as e:
        # Log the exception and continue with next object
        print(f"Detection for '{obj}' failed with exception: {e}\n")

if not found:
    print("No detections from single-object queries. Falling back to sliding_window_detection...")
    try:
        patches, patches_boxes = sliding_window_detection(image_1, ["gauge", "dial", "meter", "scale", "thermometer", "ruler"])
        if patches:
            for i, patch in enumerate(patches):
                print(f"Patch {i}:")
                display(patch.annotated_image)
                print(f"Boxes in patch {i}: {patches_boxes[i]}\n")
        else:
            print("sliding_window_detection returned no patches.")
    except Exception as e:
        print("sliding_window_detection raised an exception:", e)
